# THE V8 DESIGN CONTRACT IS FROZEN BY SHA256, SO GIT MUST NOT TOUCH ITS BYTES.
#
# `docs/design/command-center-v8/` holds three HTML files Reid approved on
# 2026-10-08. Their hashes are recorded in CONTRACT_MANIFEST.json and re-derived
# on every build by scripts/audit/contract-check.mjs (CLAUDE.md rule #36).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. This repository has
# `core.autocrlf=true` on the development machine and had no .gitattributes. The
# contract files are stored with LF. A plain `git checkout` of one of them was
# measured in this session converting it to CRLF and changing its hash:
#
#     expected c99244c31ee31ef9e7805f05f654288835dc2b56576fcc0a960381986d83c30f
#     actual   dd6546531de26e2c141f1a3b85192a1d6b01478fa3a5b402a97d69a37081c27d
#     V8 CONTRACT CHECK — FAILED
#
# So without `-text`, anyone cloning this branch on Windows gets a FAILING BUILD
# on an UNMODIFIED contract — and the obvious-looking fix would be to
# re-baseline the hashes, which is precisely the move rule #36 forbids. The
# design would then have been silently redefined by a line-ending conversion.
#
# `-text` means: never convert, in either direction, on any platform. The bytes
# in the index are the bytes on disk are the bytes that were hashed.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the contract folder. A repo-wide eol policy would
# rewrite every file in the project, which is a separate decision and not this
# one's to make.
docs/design/command-center-v8/*.html -text
docs/design/command-center-v8/*.json -text

# THE v7 PROTOTYPE AND ITS VERBATIM EXTRACT, under the same discipline and for a
# measured reason. Rule #33 records the prototype's sha256 as 37f9c4d6…112a63,
# which is the LF hash. On this machine the working copy had already been
# converted to CRLF by an earlier checkout, so it hashed 28a5e741… and RULE #33's
# OWN RECORDED HASH COULD NOT BE VERIFIED HERE AT ALL. Restoring it to LF brings
# the file back to 37f9c4d6…112a63 exactly.
#
# `v7.css` must carry the same attribute, not just the prototype.
# lib/design/v7-css.test.ts asserts that v7.css CONTAINS each of the prototype's
# four <style> blocks verbatim, which is a byte comparison across two files — so
# one being CRLF and the other LF fails it even though both are LF in the index
# and both pass on a Linux clone. Pinning both is what makes that test mean the
# same thing on every machine.
docs/design/command-center-v7/*.html -text
docs/design/command-center-v7/*.css -text

# AND THE DERIVED STYLESHEET, for the same byte-comparison reason. `pnpm css:v7`
# regenerates app/styles/command-center-v7.generated.css from v7.css, and
# lib/design/v7-css.test.ts asserts the committed file EQUALS what the transform
# produces right now. The transform's output follows its LF input, so a CRLF
# working copy of the generated file fails that equality on a Windows tree while
# passing on a Linux clone — the same split-brain the two files above had.
app/styles/command-center-v7.generated.css -text

# Generated .docx copies of specs and audits (scripts/md-to-docx.mjs). A zip
# container; any text conversion corrupts it.
*.docx binary
